long term targets is a market timing decision.

268
00:12:58,120 --> 00:13:00,620
And the returns that are attributable to

269
00:13:00,620 --> 00:13:03,120
that deviation from your long term targets

270
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:09,120
are the returns that would be attributable to market timing.

271
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:12,620
And the third source of returns

272
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:16,120
has to do with security selection.

273
00:13:16,120 --> 00:13:18,120
So,

274
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:22,120
you've got your allocation to domestic equities.

275
00:13:22,120 --> 00:13:25,120
If you buy the market, and the way that you

276
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:28,120
buy the market is to buy an index fund that

277
00:13:28,120 --> 00:13:30,620
holds all of the securities in the market

278
00:13:30,620 --> 00:13:33,120
in the proportions that they exist in the market.

279
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:35,120
If you buy the market, then

280
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:37,120
your returns to security selection are zero.

281
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:39,620
Because your portfolio is going to

282
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:42,120
perform in line with the market.

283
00:13:42,120 --> 00:13:45,120
But if you make security selection bets,

284
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:46,120
if you

285
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:49,120
decide that you want to try and beat the market,

286
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:50,120
then

287
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:53,120
that bet, or that series of bets,

288
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:58,120
will define your returns attributable to security selection.

289
00:13:58,120 --> 00:14:00,120
So, if you decide

290
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:03,120
that you think the prospects of Ford

291
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:06,120
are superior to the prospects of GM,

292
00:14:06,120 --> 00:14:09,120
well, you want to overweight Ford

293
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:11,120
and underweight GM.

294
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:13,120
And if that turns out to be

295
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:14,120
a good bet,

296
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:15,120
and you're rewarded,

297
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:18,120
because Ford outperforms and GM underperforms,

298
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:21,120
then you have a positive return to security selection.

299
00:14:21,120 --> 00:14:22,120
If the converse is true,

300
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:25,120
then you have a negative return to security selection.

301
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:27,120
But one of the really

302
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:29,120
important facts about security selection

303
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:31,120
is that

304
00:14:31,120 --> 00:14:34,120
if you play for free, it's a zero-sum game.

305
00:14:34,120 --> 00:14:37,120
Because if you've overweighted Ford and underweighted GM,

306
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:39,120
there has to be

307
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:42,120
some other investor, or group of investors,

308
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:45,120
that are underweight Ford and overweight GM.

309
00:14:45,120 --> 00:14:48,120
Because this is all relative to the market.

310
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:49,120
And so,

311
00:14:49,120 --> 00:14:52,120
if you're overweight Ford and underweight GM,

312
00:14:52,120 --> 00:14:54,120
and somebody else is

313
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:56,120
underweight Ford and overweight GM,

314
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:57,120
well, at the end of

315
00:14:57,120 --> 00:14:58,120
the day, the amount by which

316
00:14:58,120 --> 00:15:00,120
the winner wins equals the

317
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:02,120
amount by which the loser loses.

318
00:15:02,120 --> 00:15:04,120
And so it's a zero-sum game.

319
00:15:04,120 --> 00:15:06,120
But of course, if you take into account

320
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:08,120
the fact that it costs money to play the game,

321
00:15:08,120 --> 00:15:11,120
it turns into a negative-sum game.

322
00:15:11,120 --> 00:15:13,120
And the negative sum is the amount that's

323
00:15:13,120 --> 00:15:15,120
siphoned off by Wall Street.

324
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:18,120
And Wall Street takes its pound of flesh

325
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:21,120
in the form of market impact,

326
00:15:21,120 --> 00:15:23,120
in the form of commissions,

327
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:25,120
in the form of fees

328
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:27,120
that are charged

329
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:30,120
to manage the portfolio actively.

330
00:15:30,120 --> 00:15:32,120
And then sometimes there are even fees

331
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:34,120
to consultants to choose the managers.

332
00:15:34,120 --> 00:15:37,120
So there's an enormous drain from the system

333
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:42,120
that causes the active investment activity

334
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:45,120
to be a negative-sum game.

335
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:47,120
And so the question is,

336
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:50,120
what's the best way to manage the financial impact

337
00:15:50,120 --> 00:15:53,120
for those investors that decide to play?

338
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:55,120
So let's take these in turn

339
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:59,120
and start out with asset allocation.

340
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:02,120
So asset allocation is far and away

341
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:05,120
the most important tool

342
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:09,120
that we have available to us as investors.

343
00:16:09,120 --> 00:16:13,120
And when I first started thinking about this

344
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:14,120
25 years ago,

345
00:16:14,120 --> 00:16:16,120
I had a very technical law that says

346
00:16:16,120 --> 00:16:19,120
that asset allocation is the most important tool

347
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:21,120
because it seemed pretty obvious

348
00:16:21,120 --> 00:16:23,120
that that was going to be the most powerful determinant

349
00:16:23,120 --> 00:16:24,120
of returns.

350
00:16:24,120 --> 00:16:27,120
But it turns out that it's not really

351
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:29,120
a law of finance

352
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:32,120
that asset allocation dominates returns.

353
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:35,120
It's a behavioral result

354
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:38,120
of how it is that we as individual investors

355
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:41,120
or we as institutional investors

356
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:43,120
manage our portfolios.

357
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:47,120
So if I make it back to my office,

358
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:50,120
traversing these icy sidewalks,

359
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:52,120
I could go back,

360
00:16:52,120 --> 00:16:57,120
I could take Yale's $17 or $18 billion

361
00:16:57,120 --> 00:17:01,120
and put it all in Google stock.

362
00:17:01,120 --> 00:17:03,120
Now if I did that,

363
00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:05,119
I'm not sure how long I'd keep my job,

364
00:17:05,119 --> 00:17:07,119
it might be fun for a while,

365
00:17:07,119 --> 00:17:10,119
but that would probably be damaging

366
00:17:10,119 --> 00:17:12,119
to my employment prospects.

367
00:17:12,119 --> 00:17:14,119
But if I did that,

368
00:17:14,119 --> 00:17:16,119
asset allocation would have almost nothing to say

369
00:17:16,119 --> 00:17:18,119
about Yale's returns.

370
00:17:18,119 --> 00:17:21,119
It would be the idiosyncratic return

371
00:17:21,119 --> 00:17:23,119
associated with Google

372
00:17:23,119 --> 00:17:26,119
that would determine whether the endowment

373
00:17:26,119 --> 00:17:30,120
went up or down or stayed flat.

374
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:32,120
And so security selection

375
00:17:32,120 --> 00:17:35,120
would be the overwhelming

376
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:37,120
important determinant

377
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:40,120
of returns for Yale's endowment.

378
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:42,120
And if it wasn't exciting enough

379
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:44,120
to sell everything

380
00:17:44,120 --> 00:17:45,120
and put it all in Google stock,

381
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:48,120
maybe I could go back to my office

382
00:17:48,120 --> 00:17:53,120
and start day trading bond futures.

383
00:17:53,120 --> 00:17:58,120
Well, if I took Yale's entire $17 or $18 billion

384
00:17:58,120 --> 00:18:01,120
and started trading bond futures with it,

385
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:03,120
asset allocation would have very little to say

386
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:06,120
about Yale's returns.

387
00:18:06,120 --> 00:18:08,120
Security selection would probably have very little

388
00:18:08,120 --> 00:18:10,120
to say about Yale's returns.

389
00:18:10,120 --> 00:18:11,120
It would all be about

390
00:18:11,120 --> 00:18:13,120
market timing ability.

391
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:18,120
And if I'm great at following the trend,

392
00:18:18,120 --> 00:18:19,120
the trend is your friend,

393
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:21,120
of course that's true until it's not.

394
00:18:21,120 --> 00:18:26,120
Or if I've got some sort of marvelous scheme

395
00:18:26,120 --> 00:18:28,120
to outsmart all the other smart people

396
00:18:28,120 --> 00:18:31,120
who are trading in the bond market,

397
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:33,120
that could generate some nice returns,

398
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:37,120
but those returns would have nothing to do

399
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:39,120
with asset allocation,

400
00:18:39,120 --> 00:18:41,120
nothing to do with security selection,

401
00:18:41,120 --> 00:18:44,120
everything to do with market timing.

402
00:18:44,120 --> 00:18:48,120
Of course, these sound like ridiculous things, right?

403
00:18:48,120 --> 00:18:51,120
I mean, everybody in this room knows

404
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:52,120
that I'm not going to go back

405
00:18:52,120 --> 00:18:57,120
and put Yale's entire endowment in one stock.

406
00:18:57,120 --> 00:18:58,120
And they also know that

407
00:18:58,120 --> 00:18:59,120
I'm not going to go back

408
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:04,120
and day trade futures with the endowment.

409
00:19:04,120 --> 00:19:05,620
I'm going to go back

410
00:19:05,620 --> 00:19:07,120
and the portfolio's going to look

411
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:10,120
a lot like it looked yesterday and the day before

412
00:19:10,120 --> 00:19:11,120
and the month before.

413
00:19:11,120 --> 00:19:14,120
And the year before that and the year before that.

414
00:19:14,120 --> 00:19:18,120
Because as investors,

415
00:19:18,120 --> 00:19:20,120
whether we're individual investors

416
00:19:20,120 --> 00:19:21,120
or institutional investors,

417
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:26,120
we tend to have a sensible, stable approach

418
00:19:26,120 --> 00:19:29,120
to asset allocation.

419
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:35,120
And within the asset allocation framework that we employ,

420
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:39,120
we tend to hold well-diversified portfolios of securities

421
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:40,120
within each of the asset classes.

422
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:46,120
So that means that asset allocation

423
00:19:46,120 --> 00:19:51,120
is going to be the predominant determinant of returns.

424
00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:55,120
You know, Bob Shiller and I have a colleague

425
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:58,120
at the School of Management, Roger Ibbotson,

426
00:19:58,120 --> 00:20:00,120
who's done a fair amount of work

427
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:03,120
looking at the various sources of returns for investors.

428
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:05,120
And a number of years ago,

429
00:20:05,120 --> 00:20:08,120
he came out with a finding that more than 90%

430
00:20:08,120 --> 00:20:10,120
of the variability of returns

431
00:20:10,120 --> 00:20:12,120
in institutional portfolios

432
00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:15,120
had to do with the asset allocation decision.

433
00:20:15,120 --> 00:20:21,120
And that was a very widely read

434
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:25,120
and widely accepted conclusion.

435
00:20:25,120 --> 00:20:27,120
In that same study,

436
00:20:27,120 --> 00:20:30,120
I thought that there was a more interesting conclusion,

437
00:20:30,120 --> 00:20:33,120
and that was that asset allocation actually determined

438
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:38,120
more than 100% of investor returns.

439
00:20:38,120 --> 00:20:39,120
Now, how could that be?

440
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:41,120
How could asset allocation determine

441
00:20:41,120 --> 00:20:43,120
more than 100% of returns?

442
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:46,120
Well, it goes back to the discussion that we had

443
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:48,120
about security selection

444
00:20:48,120 --> 00:20:49,120
and the fact that it's

445
00:20:49,120 --> 00:20:50,120
not free to play the game.

446
00:20:50,120 --> 00:20:53,120
And the same thing is true of market timing.

447
00:20:53,120 --> 00:20:58,120
If somebody is over-weighting a particular asset class

448
00:20:58,120 --> 00:21:03,120
relative to the long-term targets that they've got,

449
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:04,120
well, there's got to be

450
00:21:04,120 --> 00:21:05,120
an offsetting position in the markets.

451
00:21:05,120 --> 00:21:07,120
Market timing is expensive in the same way

452
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:09,120
that security selection is expensive.

453
00:21:09,120 --> 00:21:12,120
And so it too is a zero-sum game,

454
00:21:12,120 --> 00:21:17,120
even though the analysis that you'd apply to market timing

455
00:21:17,120 --> 00:21:19,120
isn't quite as clear and

456
00:21:19,120 --> 00:21:21,120
crisp as in the closed system

457
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:25,120
that you've got with any individual securities market.

458
00:21:25,120 --> 00:21:31,120
So if security selection and market timing

459
00:21:31,120 --> 00:21:33,120
are negative-sum games,

460
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:37,120
then asset allocation would explain

461
00:21:37,120 --> 00:21:39,120
more than 100% of the returns.

462
00:21:39,120 --> 00:21:45,120
And on average, for the community as a whole,

463
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:48,120
because investors do engage in market timing,

464
00:21:48,120 --> 00:21:51,120
investors do engage in security selection,

465
00:21:51,120 --> 00:21:54,120
those are going to be negative-sum games,

466
00:21:54,120 --> 00:21:56,120
and you have to subtract the leakages

467
00:21:56,120 --> 00:21:59,120
occurring because of security selection and market timing

468
00:21:59,120 --> 00:22:01,120
in order to get down to

469
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:03,120
the returns that you would get

470
00:22:03,120 --> 00:22:08,120
if you just took your asset allocation targets

471
00:22:08,120 --> 00:22:11,120
and implemented them passively.

472
00:22:11,120 --> 00:22:18,120
So it turns out that asset allocation

473
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:24,120
is the most important way that we express

474
00:22:24,120 --> 00:22:31,120
our basic tenets of investment philosophy.

475
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:37,120
I talked about the importance of having an equity bias.

476
00:22:37,120 --> 00:22:43,120
Well, these are some of Roger Ibbotson's data.

477
00:22:43,120 --> 00:22:45,120
He's got this publication called

478
00:22:45,120 --> 00:22:48,120
Stocks, Bonds, Bills, and Inflation,

479
00:22:48,120 --> 00:22:50,120
although I think he might have sold it to Morningstar,

480
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:53,120
so maybe it's Morningstar's publication now.

481
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:57,120
And it actually is an outgrowth of some academic research

482
00:22:57,120 --> 00:23:00,120
that he did decades ago.

483
00:23:00,120 --> 00:23:05,120
And the basic drill was, starting in 1925,

484
00:23:05,120 --> 00:23:11,120
looking at a number of asset classes,

485
00:23:11,120 --> 00:23:13,120
the ones that I've got here are

486
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:15,120
treasury bills, treasury bonds,

487
00:23:15,120 --> 00:23:17,120
large stocks, small stocks,

488
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:21,120
and then as a benchmark, inflation.

489
00:23:21,120 --> 00:23:24,120
Starting the investment at the end of 1925,

490
00:23:24,120 --> 00:23:27,120
taking whatever income was generated from that investment,

491
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:30,120
reinvesting it, and seeing where you end up

492
00:23:30,120 --> 00:23:32,120
at the end of the period.

493
00:23:32,120 --> 00:23:35,120
And what I've got here are the numbers from 1925

494
00:23:35,120 --> 00:23:38,120
to 2009.

495
00:23:38,120 --> 00:23:40,120
And if you did that with treasury bills,

496
00:23:40,120 --> 00:23:43,120
which are short-term loans to the U.S. government,

497
00:23:43,120 --> 00:23:46,120
one of the least risky assets imaginable,

498
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:48,120
you would have ended up with 21 times your money

499
00:23:48,120 --> 00:23:49,120
over the period.

500
00:23:49,120 --> 00:23:51,120
Think about that, 21 times your money,

501
00:23:51,120 --> 00:23:53,120
that's pretty good.

502
00:23:53,120 --> 00:23:56,120
But if you think about the fact that inflation

503
00:23:56,120 --> 00:23:59,120
consumed a multiple of 12,

504
00:23:59,120 --> 00:24:02,120
well, you didn't end up with a lot after inflation.

505
00:24:02,120 --> 00:24:05,120
And if you're an institution like Yale,

506
00:24:05,120 --> 00:24:08,120
and you only want to consume after inflation returns,

507
00:24:08,120 --> 00:24:11,120
so you can maintain the purchasing power of the portfolio,

508
00:24:11,120 --> 00:24:15,120
well, 21 times, but taking off 12 times for inflation,

509
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:18,120
eh, not so good.

510
00:24:18,120 --> 00:24:20,120
One of the interesting things

511
00:24:20,120 --> 00:24:22,120
about the stocks, bonds, bills,

512
00:24:22,120 --> 00:24:24,120
and inflation numbers over long periods

513
00:24:24,120 --> 00:24:28,120
is that they correspond to our sense of the relationship

514
00:24:28,120 --> 00:24:31,120
between the riskiness of the asset

515
00:24:31,120 --> 00:24:34,120
and the notion that if you accept more risk,

516
00:24:34,120 --> 00:24:36,120
you should get higher returns.

517
00:24:36,120 --> 00:24:38,120
And so if you move out the risk spectrum,

518
00:24:38,120 --> 00:24:41,120
and instead of looking at treasury bills,

519
00:24:41,120 --> 00:24:43,120
you look at treasury bonds,

520
00:24:43,120 --> 00:24:46,120
you end up with a multiple of 86 times.

521
00:24:46,120 --> 00:24:48,120
That's pretty good, 86 times.

522
00:24:48,120 --> 00:24:50,120
I mean, it's a lot better

523
00:24:50,120 --> 00:24:52,120
than, whatever, 21 times for bills.

524
00:24:52,120 --> 00:24:55,120
But it's still not a huge return

525
00:24:55,120 --> 00:25:00,120
for decades and decades of investing.

526
00:25:00,120 --> 00:25:04,120
So what happens if you move away from lending money,

527
00:25:04,120 --> 00:25:07,120
in this case, lending money to the government,

528
00:25:07,120 --> 00:25:10,120
to owning equities?

529
00:25:10,120 --> 00:25:12,120
The multiple over this period,

530
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:16,120
and this includes the crash in 1929,

531
00:25:16,120 --> 00:25:20,120
the market collapse in 1987,

532
00:25:20,120 --> 00:25:24,120
and the most recent financial crisis,

533
00:25:24,120 --> 00:25:26,120
in spite of those blips,

534
00:25:26,120 --> 00:25:31,120
you would have ended up with 2,592 times your money.

535
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:33,120
That's stunning.

536
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:36,120
Like, way more than 86 times

537
00:25:36,120 --> 00:25:38,120
and way more than 21 times.

538
00:25:38,120 --> 00:25:40,120
So over long periods of time,

539
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:43,120
you do end up being rewarded for accepting equity risk.

540
00:25:43,120 --> 00:25:44,120
And what would have happened

541
00:25:44,120 --> 00:25:46,120
if you would have put the money in small stocks

542
00:25:46,120 --> 00:25:48,120
and let her run?

543
00:25:48,120 --> 00:25:52,120
12,226 times your money.

544
00:25:52,120 --> 00:25:55,120
So the conclusion is pretty obvious.

545
00:25:55,120 --> 00:25:58,120
This notion that if you've got a long time horizon,

546
00:25:58,120 --> 00:26:02,120
you want to expose your portfolio to equities

547
00:26:02,120 --> 00:26:05,120
makes an enormous amount of sense.

548
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:07,120
As a matter of fact,

549
00:26:07,120 --> 00:26:09,120
the first time I took a look at these numbers

550
00:26:09,120 --> 00:26:13,120
was back in 1986

551
00:26:13,120 --> 00:26:15,120
when I was teaching,

552
00:26:15,120 --> 00:26:16,620
probably a predecessor to the

553
00:26:16,620 --> 00:26:18,120
class that Bob Shiller's teaching,

554
00:26:18,120 --> 00:26:23,120
it was a lecture class in finance,

555
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:26,620
and I was preparing the

556
00:26:26,620 --> 00:26:30,120
lecture that had to do with

557
00:26:30,120 --> 00:26:32,120
long-term,

558
00:26:32,120 --> 00:26:35,120
investment philosophy.

559
00:26:35,120 --> 00:26:37,120
And that's when I first saw these numbers

560
00:26:37,120 --> 00:26:40,120
and I was a little bit disconcerted

561
00:26:40,120 --> 00:26:42,120
when I put them together because I thought,

562
00:26:42,120 --> 00:26:45,120
gee,

563
00:26:45,120 --> 00:26:46,120
21 times for bills,

564
00:26:46,120 --> 00:26:48,120
86 times for bonds,

565
00:26:48,120 --> 00:26:51,120
12,226 times for small stocks,

566
00:26:51,120 --> 00:26:53,120
maybe the right thing to do is to just

567
00:26:53,120 --> 00:26:55,120
put the whole portfolio into small stocks

568
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:58,120
and forget about it.

569
00:26:58,120 --> 00:27:00,120
And my first problem was,

570
00:27:00,120 --> 00:27:01,120
if that were true,

571
00:27:01,120 --> 00:27:02,620
what was I going to say

572
00:27:02,620 --> 00:27:04,120
for the next 10 weeks of lectures?

573
00:27:04,120 --> 00:27:07,120
My longer-term problem was

574
00:27:07,120 --> 00:27:09,120
if the investment committee figured out

575
00:27:09,120 --> 00:27:10,120
that all we needed to

576
00:27:10,120 --> 00:27:11,120
do was put the whole portfolio

577
00:27:11,120 --> 00:27:12,620
in small stocks and that that

578
00:27:12,620 --> 00:27:14,120
was the way to investment success,

579
00:27:14,120 --> 00:27:16,120
I wouldn't have a job.

580
00:27:16,120 --> 00:27:18,120
They wouldn't need me to do that.

581
00:27:18,120 --> 00:27:20,120
And I had a wife and young children

582
00:27:20,120 --> 00:27:22,120
and I like getting a paycheck

583
00:27:22,120 --> 00:27:24,120
and being able to feed and house them.

584
00:27:24,120 --> 00:27:27,120
So I took a look at the data more carefully

585
00:27:27,120 --> 00:27:30,120
and there are a number of examples

586
00:27:30,120 --> 00:27:36,120
of what it is that I'm going to talk about,

587
00:27:36,120 --> 00:27:38,120
but the most profound example

588
00:27:38,120 --> 00:27:42,120
remains around the great crash in 1929.

589
00:27:42,120 --> 00:27:44,120
And if you'd had your whole portfolio

590
00:27:44,120 --> 00:27:47,120
in small stocks at the peak,

591
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:49,120
by the end of 1929,

592
00:27:49,120 --> 00:27:52,120
you would have lost 54% of your money.

593
00:27:52,120 --> 00:27:54,120
By the end of 1930,

594
00:27:54,120 --> 00:27:57,120
you would have lost 38% of your money.

595
00:27:57,120 --> 00:27:59,120
Another 38%, that is.

596
00:27:59,120 --> 00:28:00,120
By the end of 1931,

597
00:28:00,120 --> 00:28:03,120
you would have lost another 50%.

598
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:06,120
And by June of 1932, for good measure,

599
00:28:06,120 --> 00:28:09,120
you would have lost another 32%.

600
00:28:09,120 --> 00:28:12,120
So for every dollar that you had at the peak,

601
00:28:12,120 --> 00:28:14,120
at the trough,

602
00:28:14,120 --> 00:28:16,120
you would have had 10 cents left.

603
00:28:16,120 --> 00:28:20,120
And it doesn't matter whether you're an investor

604
00:28:20,120 --> 00:28:23,120
with the strongest stomach known to mankind

605
00:28:23,120 --> 00:28:26,120
or you're an institutional investor

606
00:28:26,120 --> 00:28:29,120
with the longest investment horizon imaginable,

607
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:31,120
at some point,

608
00:28:31,120 --> 00:28:34,120
when the dollars are turning into dimes,

609
00:28:34,120 --> 00:28:36,120
they're going to say,

610
00:28:36,120 --> 00:28:39,120
this is a completely ridiculous thing

611
00:28:39,120 --> 00:28:43,120
to accept this much risk in the portfolio.

612
00:28:43,120 --> 00:28:44,120
I can't stand it.

613
00:28:44,120 --> 00:28:47,120
I'm selling all my small stocks

614
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:51,120
and going to buy treasury bonds or treasury bills, right?

615
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:55,120
And that's exactly what people did.

616
00:28:55,120 --> 00:28:58,120
And there was this sense

617
00:28:58,120 --> 00:29:01,120
in the 1930s, 1940s,

618
00:29:01,120 --> 00:29:05,120
even into the 50s and 60s,

619
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:07,120
that heavy equity exposures

620
00:29:07,120 --> 00:29:11,120
weren't a responsible thing for a fiduciary.

621
00:29:11,120 --> 00:29:13,120
When I was writing my book,

622
00:29:13,120 --> 00:29:15,120
I was kind of fooling around,

623
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:19,120
looking at articles from the Saturday Evening Post.

624
00:29:19,120 --> 00:29:21,120
And I know everybody here is too young

625
00:29:21,120 --> 00:29:24,120
to have seen the Saturday Evening Post

626
00:29:24,120 --> 00:29:27,120
when it was still publishing.

627
00:29:27,120 --> 00:29:30,120
But you've all seen Norman Rockwell prints, right?

628
00:29:30,120 --> 00:29:33,120
Well, he was famous for doing covers

629
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:34,120
for the Saturday Evening Post.

630
00:29:34,120 --> 00:29:37,120
And there was this article in the 1930s,

631
00:29:37,120 --> 00:29:39,120
that's actually before my time,

632
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:42,120
so I was looking at things in the library,

633
00:29:42,120 --> 00:29:45,120
not things that actually had been delivered to my doorstep.

634
00:29:45,120 --> 00:29:49,120
And the commentator said

635
00:29:49,120 --> 00:29:51,120
that it was ridiculous

636
00:29:51,120 --> 00:29:54,120
that stocks were called securities,

637
00:29:54,120 --> 00:29:56,120
that they were so risky

638
00:29:56,120 --> 00:29:59,120
that we should call stocks insecurities.

639
00:29:59,120 --> 00:30:03,120
There was just this visceral dislike

640
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:06,120
for the risks that were associated

641
00:30:06,120 --> 00:30:08,120
with the stock market

642
00:30:08,120 --> 00:30:11,120
because it had caused so many investors so much pain.

643
00:30:11,120 --> 00:30:15,120
So yes, stocks are a great thing

644
00:30:15,120 --> 00:30:19,120
for investors with long time horizons,

645
00:30:19,120 --> 00:30:21,120
but you need to diversify

646
00:30:21,120 --> 00:30:23,120
because you've got to be able

647
00:30:23,120 --> 00:30:25,120
to live through

648
00:30:25,120 --> 00:30:28,120
those inevitable periods

649
00:30:28,120 --> 00:30:31,120
where risky assets produce results

650
00:30:31,120 --> 00:30:37,120
that are sometimes so bad as to be frightening.

651
00:30:37,120 --> 00:30:41,120
Second source of return, market timing.

652
00:30:41,120 --> 00:30:44,120
A few years ago,

653
00:30:44,120 --> 00:30:48,120
a group of former colleagues of mine

654
00:30:48,120 --> 00:30:52,120
gave me a party at the Yale Club

655
00:30:52,120 --> 00:30:54,120
and they presented me with a copy

656
00:30:54,120 --> 00:30:58,120
of Keynes' General Theory.

657
00:30:58,120 --> 00:31:00,120
Because back when I used to teach

658
00:31:00,120 --> 00:31:02,120
a big finance class like this,

659
00:31:02,120 --> 00:31:05,120
the last class always involved

660
00:31:05,120 --> 00:31:07,120
reading from Keynes.

661
00:31:07,120 --> 00:31:10,120
And I think Keynes was one of the best authors

662
00:31:10,120 --> 00:31:14,120
about investing in financial markets,

663
00:31:14,120 --> 00:31:16,120
bar none.

664
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:19,120
I remember one of my students telling me afterwards

665
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:21,120
that I was reading from Keynes

666
00:31:21,120 --> 00:31:22,120
as if I were reading from the Bible.

667
00:31:22,120 --> 00:31:24,120
And I had this paperback copy

668
00:31:24,120 --> 00:31:26,120
that was falling apart.

669
00:31:26,120 --> 00:31:28,120
And my former students remembered this

670
00:31:28,120 --> 00:31:31,120
and they gave me this beautiful

671
00:31:31,120 --> 00:31:33,120
first edition of Keynes.

672
00:31:33,120 --> 00:31:35,120
And I was on the train back from New York

673
00:31:35,120 --> 00:31:38,120
where the party had occurred to New Haven.

674
00:31:38,120 --> 00:31:41,120
And I found this quote.

675
00:31:41,120 --> 00:31:44,120
The idea of wholesale shifts

676
00:31:44,120 --> 00:31:46,120
is for various reasons impracticable

677
00:31:46,120 --> 00:31:48,120
and indeed undesirable.

678
00:31:48,120 --> 00:31:50,120
Most of those who attempt to

679
00:31:50,120 --> 00:31:52,120
sell too late and buy too late

680
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:54,120
and do both too often increase their risk.

681
00:31:54,120 --> 00:31:56,120
And heavy expenses,

682
00:31:56,120 --> 00:31:58,120
there's that negative sum game thing,

683
00:31:58,120 --> 00:31:59,120
and developing too unsettled

684
00:31:59,120 --> 00:32:02,120
and speculative a state of mind.

685
00:32:02,120 --> 00:32:04,120
And as in most things,

686
00:32:04,120 --> 00:32:08,120
the data support Keynes' conclusions.

687
00:32:08,120 --> 00:32:10,120
Morningstar did a study

688
00:32:10,120 --> 00:32:13,120
of all of the mutual funds

689
00:32:13,120 --> 00:32:16,120
in the U.S. domestic equity market.

690
00:32:16,120 --> 00:32:19,120
And there were 17 categories of funds.

691
00:32:19,120 --> 00:32:21,120
And what they did with this study

692
00:32:21,120 --> 00:32:23,120
is they looked at 10 years of returns

693
00:32:23,120 --> 00:32:25,120
and compared dollar-weighted returns

694
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:27,120
to time-weighted returns.

695
00:32:27,120 --> 00:32:29,120
Now time-weighted returns are simply

696
00:32:29,120 --> 00:32:31,120
the returns that are generated year in and year out.

697
00:32:31,120 --> 00:32:33,120
If you get an offering memorandum or a prospectus,

698
00:32:33,120 --> 00:32:36,120
they'll show you the time-weighted return.

699
00:32:36,120 --> 00:32:38,120
If you look at the advertisements

700
00:32:38,120 --> 00:32:44,120
where Fidelity's touting its latest, greatest funds,

701
00:32:44,120 --> 00:32:48,120
the returns that you see are time-weighted returns.

702
00:32:48,120 --> 00:32:51,120
Dollar-weighted returns take into account cash flows.

703
00:32:51,120 --> 00:32:53,120
All right, so in a dollar-weighted return,

704
00:32:53,120 --> 00:32:56,120
if investors put more money into the fund

705
00:32:56,120 --> 00:32:57,120
in a particular year,

706
00:32:57,120 --> 00:33:00,120
that year's return will have a greater weight

707
00:33:00,120 --> 00:33:02,120
in the calculation.

708
00:33:02,120 --> 00:33:03,120
So here we have all

709
00:33:03,120 --> 00:33:04,120
the mutual funds in the U.S.,

710
00:33:04,120 --> 00:33:07,120
17 categories, time-weighted versus dollar-weighted.

711
00:33:07,120 --> 00:33:11,120
In every one of those categories,

712
00:33:11,120 --> 00:33:12,120
the dollar-weighted returns

713
00:33:12,120 --> 00:33:15,120
were less than the time-weighted returns.

714
00:33:15,120 --> 00:33:16,120
What does that mean?

715
00:33:16,120 --> 00:33:20,120
That means that investors systematically made

716
00:33:20,120 --> 00:33:22,120
perverse decisions

717
00:33:22,120 --> 00:33:24,120
as to when to invest

718
00:33:24,120 --> 00:33:27,120
and when to disinvest from mutual funds.

719
00:33:27,120 --> 00:33:31,120
What investors were doing,

720
00:33:31,120 --> 00:33:36,120
they were buying in after a fund

721
00:33:36,120 --> 00:33:39,120
had shown strong relative performance

722
00:33:39,120 --> 00:33:42,120
and selling after a fund

723
00:33:42,120 --> 00:33:45,120
had shown poor relative performance.

724
00:33:45,120 --> 00:33:48,120
So they were systematically buying high and selling low.

725
00:33:48,120 --> 00:33:51,120
And it doesn't matter whether you do that

726
00:33:51,120 --> 00:33:54,120
with great enthusiasm and in great volume,

727
00:33:54,120 --> 00:33:56,120
it's a really, really bad way to make money.

728
00:33:56,120 --> 00:33:58,120
Very difficult.

729
00:33:58,120 --> 00:34:05,120
So the conclusion for these individuals

730
00:34:05,120 --> 00:34:08,120
that operate in the mutual fund market is

731
00:34:08,120 --> 00:34:11,120
that their market timing decisions

732
00:34:11,120 --> 00:34:14,120
were systematically perverse.

733
00:34:14,120 --> 00:34:16,620
I also took a look

734
00:34:16,620 --> 00:34:19,120
at the top 10 internet funds

735
00:34:19,120 --> 00:34:21,120
during the tech bubble.

736
00:34:21,120 --> 00:34:23,120
This is something I published in my book

737
00:34:23,120 --> 00:34:26,120
for individual investors.

738
00:34:26,120 --> 00:34:29,120
And if you looked at the top 10 internet funds

739
00:34:29,120 --> 00:34:31,120
three years before and three years after the bubble,

740
00:34:31,120 --> 00:34:35,120
the time-weighted return was 1.5% per year.

741
00:34:35,120 --> 00:34:37,120
If you look at that and you say,

742
00:34:37,120 --> 00:34:38,120
1.5% per year,

743
00:34:38,120 --> 00:34:40,120
well, the market went way up and way down,

744
00:34:40,120 --> 00:34:42,120
but 1.5% per year, that's not so bad.

745
00:34:42,120 --> 00:34:45,120
No harm, no foul.

746
00:34:45,120 --> 00:34:49,120
Investors invested $13.7 billion

747
00:34:49,120 --> 00:34:51,120
and lost $9.5 billion.

748
00:34:51,120 --> 00:34:55,120
So they lost 72% of what they invested.

749
00:34:55,120 --> 00:34:56,120
How could it be that

750
00:34:56,120 --> 00:34:57,120
they lost 72% of the money

751
00:34:57,120 --> 00:35:00,120
that they invested when the time-weighted return

752
00:35:00,120 --> 00:35:03,120
was 1.5% per year for six years?

753
00:35:03,120 --> 00:35:06,120
Well, they weren't invested in the internet funds in 1997,

754
00:35:06,120 --> 00:35:09,120
and they weren't invested in 1998,

755
00:35:09,120 --> 00:35:11,120
and they weren't invested in early 1999.

756
00:35:11,120 --> 00:35:14,120
It was in late 1999 and early 2000

757
00:35:14,120 --> 00:35:18,120
that all the money piled in at the very top.

758
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:20,120
And then in 2001 and 2002,

759
00:35:20,120 --> 00:35:23,120
bitterly disappointed, they sold.

760
00:35:23,120 --> 00:35:26,120
So they lost 72% of what they put in,

761
00:35:26,120 --> 00:35:28,120
even though the time-weighted returns

762
00:35:28,120 --> 00:35:32,120
were 1.5% per year positive.

763
00:35:32,120 --> 00:35:39,120
So institutions don't get a free pass either.

764
00:35:39,120 --> 00:35:44,120
If you look at the crash in October 1987,

765
00:35:44,120 --> 00:35:47,120
which was an extraordinary event,

766
00:35:47,120 --> 00:35:49,120
I think the capitalists,

767
00:35:49,120 --> 00:35:51,120
I think the calculation I did

768
00:35:51,120 --> 00:35:53,120
put it at a 25 standard deviation event,

769
00:35:53,120 --> 00:35:57,120
which is essentially an impossibility.

770
00:35:57,120 --> 00:35:59,120
But however you measure it,

771
00:35:59,120 --> 00:36:01,120
it was an extraordinary event.

772
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:04,120
And what happened on October 19, 1987?

773
00:36:04,120 --> 00:36:06,120
Well, stock markets the world around

774
00:36:06,120 --> 00:36:09,120
went down by more than 20%.

775
00:36:09,120 --> 00:36:11,120
What people forget is,

776
00:36:11,120 --> 00:36:13,120
along with the stock markets going down,

777
00:36:13,120 --> 00:36:15,120
there was a huge rally in government bonds,

778
00:36:15,120 --> 00:36:17,120
flight to safety.

779
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:18,120
So stocks were cheaper,

780
00:36:18,120 --> 00:36:19,120
bonds were more expensive,

781
00:36:19,120 --> 00:36:21,120
and so were the bonds.

782
00:36:21,120 --> 00:36:23,120
What did institutional investors do?

783
00:36:23,120 --> 00:36:26,120
Well, they got scared and they sold stocks

784
00:36:26,120 --> 00:36:28,120
and bought bonds.

785
00:36:28,120 --> 00:36:31,120
Same thing, buying high, selling low.

786
00:36:31,120 --> 00:36:33,120
As a matter of fact,

787
00:36:33,120 --> 00:36:37,120
endowments took six years

788
00:36:37,120 --> 00:36:43,120
to get their post-crash equity allocations

789
00:36:43,120 --> 00:36:46,120
back up to where they were before the crash,

790
00:36:46,120 --> 00:36:48,120
arguably underweighted in equities

791
00:36:48,120 --> 00:36:51,120
and the heart of one of the greatest bull markets

792
00:36:51,120 --> 00:36:53,120
of all time.

793
00:36:53,120 --> 00:36:56,120
So it seems that investors,

794
00:36:56,120 --> 00:36:59,120
whether they're individual or institutional,

795
00:36:59,120 --> 00:37:03,120
have this perverse predilection

796
00:37:03,120 --> 00:37:05,120
to chasing performance,

797
00:37:05,120 --> 00:37:07,120
buying something after it's gone up,

798
00:37:07,120 --> 00:37:10,120
selling something after it's gone down,

799
00:37:10,120 --> 00:37:12,120
and using market timing

800
00:37:12,120 --> 00:37:16,120
to damage portfolio returns.

801
00:37:16,120 --> 00:37:18,120
The final tool that we have available today

802
00:37:18,120 --> 00:37:21,120
to us as investors is security selection.

803
00:37:21,120 --> 00:37:25,120
I cite a study in my book,

804
00:37:25,120 --> 00:37:27,120
Unconventional Success,

805
00:37:27,120 --> 00:37:29,120
conducted by Rob Arnott,

806
00:37:29,120 --> 00:37:31,120
that does a very good job

807
00:37:31,120 --> 00:37:34,120
at looking at 20 years' worth of mutual fund returns,

808
00:37:34,120 --> 00:37:38,120
and he says that there's about a 14% chance

809
00:37:38,120 --> 00:37:41,120
that, or historically,

810
00:37:41,120 --> 00:37:44,120
there was a 14% chance of beating the market

811
00:37:44,120 --> 00:37:47,120
after adjusting for fees and taxes.

812
00:37:47,120 --> 00:37:50,120
So you'd think zero-sum game

813
00:37:50,120 --> 00:37:53,120
would be a coin flip 50-50,

814
00:37:53,120 --> 00:37:56,120
but because of the leakages from the system

815
00:37:56,120 --> 00:37:58,120
and because of taxes,

816
00:37:58,120 --> 00:38:01,120
the probability of winning goes down to 14%.

817
00:38:01,120 --> 00:38:03,120
But oh, by the way,

818
00:38:03,120 --> 00:38:07,120
that 14% ignores two very important things.

819
00:38:07,120 --> 00:38:11,120
One is that a huge percentage of mutual funds

820
00:38:11,120 --> 00:38:13,120
have front-end loads.

821
00:38:13,120 --> 00:38:15,120
If you call your friendly broker

822
00:38:15,120 --> 00:38:17,120
to buy a mutual fund,

823
00:38:17,120 --> 00:38:19,620
they'll extract a payment of 2 or

824
00:38:19,620 --> 00:38:22,120
3 or 4 or 5 or 6%.

825
00:38:22,120 --> 00:38:24,120
Those numbers aren't included,

826
00:38:24,120 --> 00:38:25,120
so if you included the loads,

827
00:38:25,120 --> 00:38:27,120
that would make the likelihood of winning

828
00:38:27,120 --> 00:38:29,120
substantially less than 14%,

829
00:38:29,120 --> 00:38:31,120
but even more important

830
00:38:31,120 --> 00:38:33,120
is the concept of survivorship bias.

831
00:38:33,120 --> 00:38:36,120
If you look at 20 years' worth of returns,

832
00:38:36,120 --> 00:38:38,120
the only returns that you can look at

833
00:38:38,120 --> 00:38:39,620
are the returns of the

834
00:38:39,620 --> 00:38:41,120
funds that survived for 20 years.

835
00:38:41,120 --> 00:38:44,120
Well, which funds didn't survive?

836
00:38:44,120 --> 00:38:46,120
Almost always the funds

837
00:38:46,120 --> 00:38:49,120
that don't survive are the failures.

838
00:38:49,120 --> 00:38:51,120
So you're only looking at the winners.

839
00:38:51,120 --> 00:38:53,120
If you look at the winners

840
00:38:53,120 --> 00:38:56,120
and you only have a 14% chance,

841
00:38:56,120 --> 00:38:59,120
if you take into account the losers,

842
00:38:59,120 --> 00:39:03,120
that 14% chance has to go to essentially zero.

843
00:39:03,120 --> 00:39:07,120
And is survivorship bias an important phenomenon?

844
00:39:07,120 --> 00:39:08,120
It is.

845
00:39:08,120 --> 00:39:13,120
The Center for Research on Securities Prices

846
00:39:13,120 --> 00:39:15,120
has a survivorship bias-free

847
00:39:15,120 --> 00:39:18,120
U.S. mutual fund database,

848
00:39:18,120 --> 00:39:23,120
meaning that it tracks the funds that fail.

849
00:39:23,120 --> 00:39:27,120
There were 30,361 funds in the database.

850
00:39:27,120 --> 00:39:31,120
19,129 were living.

851
00:39:31,120 --> 00:39:35,120
11,232 were dead.

852
00:39:35,120 --> 00:39:37,120
So almost a,

853
00:39:37,120 --> 00:39:40,120
or more than a third of the funds

854
00:39:40,120 --> 00:39:45,120
in this survivorship bias-free database

855
00:39:45,120 --> 00:39:47,120
were ones that had died.

856
00:39:47,120 --> 00:39:51,120
And they died mostly because they failed.

857
00:39:51,120 --> 00:39:53,120
And that's kind of an honorable way to die.

858
00:39:53,120 --> 00:39:55,120
There are other ways to die.

859
00:39:55,120 --> 00:39:58,120
If you're a big mutual fund complex like Fidelity,

860
00:39:58,120 --> 00:40:01,120
and you've got an underperforming fund,

861
00:40:01,120 --> 00:40:04,120
what you tend to do is something like,

862
00:40:04,120 --> 00:40:06,120
well, let's merge that into this fund

863
00:40:06,120 --> 00:40:07,120
that has good performance.

864
00:40:07,120 --> 00:40:09,120
And guess what happens?

865
00:40:09,120 --> 00:40:11,120
Fidelity loses a fund that has bad performance,

866
00:40:11,120 --> 00:40:13,120
and the one that has good performance

867
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:14,120
has more assets,

868
00:40:14,120 --> 00:40:17,120
and as they merge the underperforming fund into it,

869
00:40:17,120 --> 00:40:19,120
it makes them look like they're

870
00:40:19,120 --> 00:40:22,120
a more successful fund management firm.

871
00:40:22,120 --> 00:40:26,120
There's one other aspect of security selection

872
00:40:26,120 --> 00:40:28,120
that's important.

873
00:40:28,120 --> 00:40:32,120
An aspect other than the fact that

874
00:40:32,120 --> 00:40:34,120
it's a negative sum game,

875
00:40:34,120 --> 00:40:42,120
very tough for practitioners to win.

876
00:40:42,120 --> 00:40:44,120
And that has to do with the degree

877
00:40:44,120 --> 00:40:47,120
of opportunity that you've got in various asset classes.

878
00:40:47,120 --> 00:40:49,120
A number of years ago,

879
00:40:49,120 --> 00:40:54,120
I wanted to come up with a way of identifying,

880
00:40:54,120 --> 00:40:57,120
in an analytical manner,

881
00:40:57,120 --> 00:40:59,120
where it is that we could find

882
00:40:59,120 --> 00:41:01,120
the most attractive investment opportunities.

883
00:41:01,120 --> 00:41:03,120
And as far as I know,

884
00:41:03,120 --> 00:41:05,120
financial economists haven't determined

885
00:41:05,120 --> 00:41:10,120
a way to directly measure

886
00:41:10,120 --> 00:41:13,120
how efficient individual markets are.

887
00:41:14,120 --> 00:41:17,120
So I took a look at distributions of returns

888
00:41:17,120 --> 00:41:20,120
for various asset classes,

889
00:41:20,120 --> 00:41:22,120
and I had this notion

890
00:41:22,120 --> 00:41:26,120
that if a market priced assets efficiently,

891
00:41:26,120 --> 00:41:30,120
the distribution of returns around

892
00:41:30,120 --> 00:41:32,120
the market return would be very tight.

893
00:41:32,120 --> 00:41:34,120
Now why would that be?

894
00:41:34,120 --> 00:41:36,120
Well, if somebody makes a big bet

895
00:41:36,120 --> 00:41:38,120
in an efficient market,

896
00:41:38,120 --> 00:41:43,120
by definition, whether that bet succeeds or fails,

897
00:41:43,120 --> 00:41:48,120
has to do with more luck than sense.

898
00:41:48,120 --> 00:41:50,120
Because the premise is that these assets

899
00:41:50,120 --> 00:41:52,120
are efficiently priced,

900
00:41:52,120 --> 00:41:53,120
and you don't make a

901
00:41:53,120 --> 00:41:54,120
big win on a big bet

902
00:41:54,120 --> 00:41:57,120
unless there's an inefficiency that you're exploiting.

903
00:41:57,120 --> 00:42:00,120
So if you're making big bets

904
00:42:00,120 --> 00:42:02,120
in an efficiently priced market,

905
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:04,120
you might win one year and gather more assets,

906
00:42:04,120 --> 00:42:06,120
and you might win another year and gather more assets,

907
00:42:06,120 --> 00:42:08,120
but ultimately your luck's going to run out,

908
00:42:08,120 --> 00:42:10,120
and you're going to fail,

909
00:42:10,120 --> 00:42:11,120
and then people will fire you,

910
00:42:11,120 --> 00:42:12,120
and you'll lose your assets,

911
00:42:12,120 --> 00:42:15,120
and lose your income stream.

912
00:42:15,120 --> 00:42:17,120
So the right thing to

913
00:42:17,120 --> 00:42:19,120
do in an efficiently priced market

914
00:42:19,120 --> 00:42:22,120
is to hug the benchmark.

915
00:42:22,120 --> 00:42:24,120
People call it closet indexing.

916
00:42:24,120 --> 00:42:26,120
Look like everybody else,

917
00:42:26,120 --> 00:42:29,120
and, you know, we're human beings.

918
00:42:29,120 --> 00:42:31,120
We don't like firing people.

919
00:42:31,120 --> 00:42:34,120
We don't like admitting we're wrong.

920
00:42:34,120 --> 00:42:38,120
And so if somebody has kind of market-like performance,

921
00:42:38,120 --> 00:42:40,120
and maybe it's not all that outstanding,

922
00:42:40,120 --> 00:42:41,120
say, okay, fine,

923
00:42:41,120 --> 00:42:44,120
we'll just continue with

924
00:42:44,120 --> 00:42:47,120
this particular investment strategy,

925
00:42:47,120 --> 00:42:50,120
even though it's not doing great things,

926
00:42:50,120 --> 00:42:52,120
at least it's not doing terrible things.

927
00:42:52,120 --> 00:42:55,120
On the other end of the spectrum,

928
00:42:55,120 --> 00:42:57,120
maybe there's not even a market

929
00:42:57,120 --> 00:43:02,120
that you can match with your investment strategy.

930
00:43:02,120 --> 00:43:05,120
I mean, think about venture capital.

931
00:43:05,120 --> 00:43:06,620
I mean, how is it

932
00:43:06,620 --> 00:43:08,120
that you could index venture capital?

933
00:43:08,120 --> 00:43:09,120
You can't.

934
00:43:09,120 --> 00:43:11,120
It's a bunch of private partnerships

935
00:43:11,120 --> 00:43:15,120
and a bunch of idiosyncratic enterprises,

936
00:43:15,120 --> 00:43:17,120
and even if you wanted to,

937
00:43:17,120 --> 00:43:19,120
you couldn't match the market.

938
00:43:19,120 --> 00:43:22,120
So you're forced to go out

939
00:43:22,120 --> 00:43:25,120
and forge your own path

940
00:43:25,120 --> 00:43:29,120
and live and die by the decisions that you make.

941
00:43:29,120 --> 00:43:32,120
So how does this kind of thought piece

942
00:43:32,120 --> 00:43:35,120
translate into real numbers?

943
00:43:35,120 --> 00:43:38,120
So again, we're looking at 10 years' worth of returns

944
00:43:38,120 --> 00:43:40,120
for various asset classes.

945
00:43:40,120 --> 00:43:42,120
I look at the difference between the top quartile manager

946
00:43:42,120 --> 00:43:44,120
and the bottom quartile,

947
00:43:44,120 --> 00:43:46,120
the difference between first and third quartile.

948
00:43:46,120 --> 00:43:48,120
You could use any measure of distribution that you want.

949
00:43:48,120 --> 00:43:50,120
And in the bond market,

950
00:43:50,120 --> 00:43:51,620
which is probably the most

951
00:43:51,620 --> 00:43:53,120
efficiently priced of all markets,

952
00:43:53,120 --> 00:43:55,120
and the reason it's most efficiently priced

953
00:43:55,120 --> 00:43:58,120
is because bonds are just math, right?

954
00:43:58,120 --> 00:44:01,120
You've got coupons, you've got principal,

955
00:44:01,120 --> 00:44:04,120
you've got probabilities of default.

956
00:44:04,120 --> 00:44:06,120
It's the most easily analyzed

957
00:44:06,120 --> 00:44:08,120
of all the assets in which we invest.

958
00:44:08,120 --> 00:44:11,120
The difference between top quartile and bottom quartile

959
00:44:11,120 --> 00:44:15,120
is a half a percent per annum, almost nothing.

960
00:44:15,120 --> 00:44:18,120
All bond managers are jammed together

961
00:44:18,120 --> 00:44:21,120
right in the heart of the distribution

962
00:44:21,120 --> 00:44:25,120
because if they were out there making crazy bets

963
00:44:25,120 --> 00:44:28,120
and generating returns that were fundamentally different

964
00:44:28,120 --> 00:44:30,120
from the market,

965
00:44:30,120 --> 00:44:32,120
they'd be in that category of,

966
00:44:32,120 --> 00:44:34,120
yeah, sure, it's great when it works,

967
00:44:34,120 --> 00:44:36,120
but when it doesn't, you're dead.

968
00:44:36,120 --> 00:44:38,120
Large cap stocks,

969
00:44:38,120 --> 00:44:40,120
less efficiently priced than bonds,

970
00:44:40,120 --> 00:44:42,120
but still pretty efficiently priced,

971
00:44:42,120 --> 00:44:44,120
two percentage points per annum difference,

972
00:44:44,120 --> 00:44:46,120
first to third quartile over ten years.

973
00:44:46,120 --> 00:44:48,120
Foreign stocks, less efficiently priced

974
00:44:48,120 --> 00:44:50,120
than those in the domestic markets,

975
00:44:50,120 --> 00:44:52,120
four points per year.

976
00:44:52,120 --> 00:44:55,120
Then you move into the hedge fund world,

977
00:44:55,120 --> 00:44:57,120
the part of the hedge fund world that we call

978
00:44:57,120 --> 00:44:59,120
absolute return at Yale,

979
00:44:59,120 --> 00:45:03,120
7.1 percentage points first to third quartile.

980
00:45:03,120 --> 00:45:05,120
Real estate, much less efficiently priced

981
00:45:05,120 --> 00:45:07,120
than marketable securities,

982
00:45:07,120 --> 00:45:10,120
three percentage points top to bottom quartile.

983
00:45:10,120 --> 00:45:13,120
Leverage buyouts,

984
00:45:13,120 --> 00:45:16,120
13.7 percent difference,

985
00:45:16,120 --> 00:45:18,120
top quartile to bottom quartile.

986
00:45:18,120 --> 00:45:21,120
And the venture capital,

987
00:45:21,120 --> 00:45:24,120
43.2 percentage points difference,

988
00:45:24,120 --> 00:45:26,120
top to bottom quartile.

989
00:45:26,120 --> 00:45:30,120
So the measure that we have here

990
00:45:30,120 --> 00:45:33,120
of market inefficiency points us

991
00:45:33,120 --> 00:45:36,120
toward spending our time and energy

992
00:45:36,120 --> 00:45:39,120
trying to find the best venture capital managers,

993
00:45:39,120 --> 00:45:42,120
trying to find the best leverage buyout managers,

994
00:45:42,120 --> 00:45:46,120
and spending far less of our time and energy

995
00:45:46,120 --> 00:45:48,120
trying to beat the bond market

996
00:45:48,120 --> 00:45:49,120
or beat the stock market,

997
00:45:49,120 --> 00:45:51,120
because even if you win there,

998
00:45:51,120 --> 00:45:53,120
and even if you end up in the top quartile,

999
00:45:53,120 --> 00:45:56,120
you're not adding an enormous amount of value

1000
00:45:56,120 --> 00:45:58,120
relative to what you would have had

1001
00:45:58,120 --> 00:46:02,120
if you just would have bought the market.

1002
00:46:02,120 --> 00:46:04,120
So with that background,

1003
00:46:04,120 --> 00:46:06,120
let's revisit

1004
00:46:06,120 --> 00:46:09,120
the criticisms that Barron's leveled

1005
00:46:09,120 --> 00:46:13,120
at the Yale Model and the Swenson approach.

1006
00:46:13,120 --> 00:46:16,120
First of all, they talk about diversification failing.

1007
00:46:16,120 --> 00:46:21,120
And the fact is that in a panic,

1008
00:46:21,120 --> 00:46:24,120
only two things matter,

1009
00:46:24,120 --> 00:46:27,120
risk and safety.

1010
00:46:27,120 --> 00:46:30,120
And I saw this in 1987,

1011
00:46:30,120 --> 00:46:34,120
saw it in 1998 with the collapse of long-term capital,

1012
00:46:34,120 --> 00:46:36,120
and saw it in 2008,

1013
00:46:36,120 --> 00:46:37,620
and saw it in a

1014
00:46:37,620 --> 00:46:39,120
way that was even more profound

1015
00:46:39,120 --> 00:46:41,120
than in 87 and 98.

1016
00:46:41,120 --> 00:46:44,120
Investors sold everything

1017
00:46:44,120 --> 00:46:46,120
that had risk associated with it

1018
00:46:46,120 --> 00:46:49,120
to buy U.S. Treasuries.

1019
00:46:49,120 --> 00:46:51,120
Safety was all that mattered.

1020
00:46:51,120 --> 00:46:53,120
And of course,

1021
00:46:53,120 --> 00:46:57,120
in that narrow window of time,

1022
00:46:57,120 --> 00:47:00,120
diversification does fail.

1023
00:47:00,120 --> 00:47:04,120
The only diversification that would matter in that instance

1024
00:47:04,120 --> 00:47:07,120
is owning U.S. Treasuries.

1025
00:47:07,120 --> 00:47:09,120
But if you owned a

1026
00:47:09,120 --> 00:47:11,120
substantial amount of U.S. Treasury bonds,

1027
00:47:11,120 --> 00:47:13,120
and what's a substantial amount?

1028
00:47:13,120 --> 00:47:16,120
25, 30, 35% of your portfolio,

1029
00:47:16,120 --> 00:47:20,120
then under normal circumstances,

1030
00:47:20,120 --> 00:47:22,620
under the circumstances in which

1031
00:47:22,620 --> 00:47:25,120
we live most of our lives,

1032
00:47:25,120 --> 00:47:28,120
you're paying a huge opportunity cost.

1033
00:47:28,120 --> 00:47:30,620
So you could have a

1034
00:47:30,620 --> 00:47:33,120
portfolio with 30% in U.S. Treasuries,

1035
00:47:33,120 --> 00:47:35,120
and year in and year out,

1036
00:47:35,120 --> 00:47:38,120
you would pay this opportunity cost,

1037
00:47:38,120 --> 00:47:40,120
and then when the crisis comes,

1038
00:47:40,120 --> 00:47:42,120
you can be happy for

1039
00:47:42,120 --> 00:47:44,120
6 or 12 or 18 months,

1040
00:47:44,120 --> 00:47:47,120
and then you go back to paying the opportunity cost.

1041
00:47:47,120 --> 00:47:49,620
And I would argue that

1042
00:47:49,620 --> 00:47:52,120
if you expand your time horizon

1043
00:47:52,120 --> 00:47:57,120
to a sensible length of time,

1044
00:47:57,120 --> 00:48:02,120
that the strategy where you hold relatively little

1045
00:48:02,120 --> 00:48:05,120
in the high opportunity cost U.S. Treasuries

1046
00:48:05,120 --> 00:48:10,120
is the best strategy for a long-term investor.

1047
00:48:10,120 --> 00:48:13,120
And there are those who say that,

1048
00:48:13,120 --> 00:48:14,620
well, if diversification doesn't protect

1049
00:48:14,620 --> 00:48:16,120
you in times of crisis,

1050
00:48:16,120 --> 00:48:17,120
what does it matter?

1051
00:48:17,120 --> 00:48:19,120
Why would you want to diversify?

1052
00:48:19,120 --> 00:48:22,120
Well, think about Japan.

1053
00:48:22,120 --> 00:48:27,120
If you were a local Japanese investor,

1054
00:48:27,120 --> 00:48:29,120
you wanted to have an equity bias in your portfolio,

1055
00:48:29,120 --> 00:48:31,120
so you owned lots of Japanese stuff,

1056
00:48:31,120 --> 00:48:33,120
lots of Japanese stocks.

1057
00:48:33,120 --> 00:48:36,120
In 1989, at the end of the year,

1058
00:48:36,120 --> 00:48:40,120
the Nikkei closed at about 38,000.

1059
00:48:40,120 --> 00:48:43,120
At the end of 2009, 20 years later,

1060
00:48:43,120 --> 00:48:46,120
the Nikkei closed at 10,500.

1061
00:48:46,120 --> 00:48:49,120
So with your long time horizon

1062
00:48:49,120 --> 00:48:52,120
and equity bias in your portfolio over two decades,

1063
00:48:52,120 --> 00:48:55,120
you would have lost 73%.

1064
00:48:55,120 --> 00:49:00,120
So diversification makes an enormous amount of sense

1065
00:49:00,120 --> 00:49:02,120
in the long run,

1066
00:49:02,120 --> 00:49:06,120
even if there are occasional panics

1067
00:49:06,120 --> 00:49:10,120
where you're disappointed that the diversified approach

1068
00:49:10,120 --> 00:49:14,120
that you had to managing the portfolio

1069
00:49:14,120 --> 00:49:17,120
didn't produce results.

1070
00:49:17,120 --> 00:49:20,120
The second criticism, over-emphasis and alternatives,

1071
00:49:20,120 --> 00:49:23,120
let's just look at the last decade in Yale's portfolio.

1072
00:49:23,120 --> 00:49:27,120
Over the 10 years ended June 30, 2010,

1073
00:49:27,120 --> 00:49:29,120
domestic equities produced returns

1074
00:49:29,120 --> 00:49:32,120
of negative 0.7% per year.

1075
00:49:32,120 --> 00:49:37,120
Bonds produced returns of 5.9% per year.

1076
00:49:37,120 --> 00:49:39,120
Let's look at the alternatives

1077
00:49:39,120 --> 00:49:43,120
as opposed to domestic marketable securities.

1078
00:49:43,120 --> 00:49:45,120
Private equity, 6.2% per year.

1079
00:49:45,120 --> 00:49:47,120
Real estate, 6.9% per year.

1080
00:49:47,120 --> 00:49:50,120
Absolute return, 11.1% per year.

1081
00:49:50,120 --> 00:49:52,120
Timber, 12.1% per year.

1082
00:49:52,120 --> 00:49:55,120
And oil and gas, 24.7% per year.

1083
00:49:55,120 --> 00:49:58,120
I think the numbers speak

1084
00:49:58,120 --> 00:50:00,120
for themselves.

1085
00:50:00,120 --> 00:50:04,120
If you have a sensibly long time horizon,

1086
00:50:04,120 --> 00:50:07,120
these basic principles of equity orientation

1087
00:50:07,120 --> 00:50:11,120
and diversification make an enormous amount of sense.

1088
00:50:11,120 --> 00:50:15,120
And if you look at the bottom line,

1089
00:50:15,120 --> 00:50:17,120
which is performance,

1090
00:50:17,120 --> 00:50:23,120
when I began managing Yale's endowment in 1985,

